### Step 3: The Ansible Roles (The "How")

Roles break our logic into manageable, reusable components.

#### File: `roles/virtualbox_create/tasks/main.yml`

```yaml
# roles/virtualbox_create/tasks/main.yml
# ---
# Description: This role handles the creation of a new VirtualBox VM.
#              It clones from a base VM, configures hardware, and ensures
#              it is running. All tasks are delegated to localhost because
#              VirtualBox is managed from the Ansible control node.

- name: "CREATE | Clone VM from '{{ base_vm_name }}'"
  # Description: Creates the VM by cloning a pre-existing base image.
  # This is significantly faster than building from an ISO.
  community.general.virtualbox_vm:
    name: "{{ inventory_hostname }}"
    clone: "{{ base_vm_name }}"
    state: running
    cpus: "{{ cpu }}"
    memory: "{{ memory_mb }}"
    # Defines a single network interface with the mode from inventory.
    nics:
      - nic1: "{{ network_mode }}"
        bridgeadapter: "{{ bridge_adapter if network_mode == 'bridged' else omit }}"
        hostonlyadapter: "{{ hostonly_adapter if network_mode == 'hostonly' else omit }}"
  delegate_to: localhost

- name: "CREATE | Attach an additional data disk if defined"
  # Description: Creates and attaches a new virtual disk (VDI) to the VM.
  # This disk will be used by the 'lvm_resize' role.
  community.general.vboxmanage:
    command: "createmedium disk --filename {{ inventory_hostname }}_data.vdi --size {{ data_disk_size_mb }} && storageattach {{ inventory_hostname }} --storagectl SATA --port 1 --type hdd --medium {{ inventory_hostname }}_data.vdi"
  delegate_to: localhost
  when: data_disk_size_mb is defined

- name: "CREATE | Wait for guest OS to boot and SSH to become available"
  # Description: Pauses the playbook until the VM has fully booted and
  # Ansible can establish an SSH connection to it. This is critical for
  # running subsequent tasks inside the guest.
  ansible.builtin.wait_for_connection:
    delay: 15
    timeout: 300
    # The inventory_hostname is the name of the VM, but here we tell ansible to
    # connect to the IP address we defined in our inventory.
    ansible_host: "{{ ip_address }}"

- name: "CREATE | Add host to local SSH configuration for easy access"
  # Description: Creates an entry in the user's ~/.ssh/config file, allowing
  # you to connect with a simple 'ssh vm01' command.
  community.general.ssh_config:
    user: "{{ lookup('env', 'USER') }}"
    remote_user: "{{ ansible_user }}"
    host: "{{ inventory_hostname }}"
    hostname: "{{ ip_address }}"
    state: present
  throttle: 1
  delegate_to: localhost
```

#### File: `roles/virtualbox_delete/tasks/main.yml`

```yaml
# roles/virtualbox_delete/tasks/main.yml
# ---
# Description: This role handles the complete removal of a VirtualBox VM.
#              It powers off the machine, unregisters it, and deletes all
#              associated files, including virtual disks.

- name: "DELETE | Confirm user wants to delete the VM"
  # Description: A safety check to prevent accidental deletion. The play
  # will only proceed if the user types 'yes'.
  ansible.builtin.pause:
    prompt: "Are you absolutely sure you want to delete the VM '{{ inventory_hostname }}' and all its data? (yes/no)"
  register: confirm_delete
  run_once: true # Ensures this question is asked only once per playbook run.

- name: "DELETE | Power off and unregister the VirtualBox VM"
  # Description: Setting the state to 'absent' tells the module to
  # stop the VM and completely delete it from VirtualBox and the filesystem.
  community.general.virtualbox_vm:
    name: "{{ inventory_hostname }}"
    state: absent
  delegate_to: localhost
  when: confirm_delete.user_input | lower == 'yes'

- name: "DELETE | Remove host entry from local SSH configuration"
  # Description: Cleans up the ~/.ssh/config file by removing the entry
  # for the deleted VM.
  community.general.ssh_config:
    user: "{{ lookup('env', 'USER') }}"
    host: "{{ inventory_hostname }}"
    state: absent
  throttle: 1
  delegate_to: localhost
  when: confirm_delete.user_input | lower == 'yes'
```

#### File: `roles/lvm_resize/tasks/main.yml`

```yaml
# roles/lvm_resize/tasks/main.yml
# ---
# Description: This advanced role runs *inside the guest VM* after creation.
#              It partitions a secondary disk (/dev/sdb), adds it to a
#              volume group, and extends a logical volume to use the new space.
#              This requires 'become: true' as these are privileged operations.

- name: "RESIZE | Install LVM and partitioning tools"
  # Description: Ensures the necessary OS packages are present.
  ansible.builtin.package:
    name:
      - lvm2
      - parted
    state: present
  become: true # Run this task as root (sudo).

- name: "RESIZE | Create a new primary partition on the data disk (/dev/sdb)"
  # Description: Formats the entire second disk (/dev/sdb) as a single
  # partition flagged for use by LVM.
  community.general.parted:
    device: /dev/sdb
    number: 1
    flags: [ lvm ]
    state: present
    part_end: "100%"
  become: true
  register: new_partition

- name: "RESIZE | Extend the volume group with the new partition"
  # Description: Adds the newly created partition to the existing volume group.
  # This assumes a volume group named 'ubuntu-vg' exists (default for Ubuntu Server).
  community.general.lvg:
    vg: ubuntu-vg
    pvs: /dev/sdb1
    state: present
  become: true
  when: new_partition.changed # Only run if the partition was newly created.

- name: "RESIZE | Extend the logical volume to use all free space"
  # Description: Resizes the root logical volume to occupy 100% of the newly
  # added free space in the volume group, and resizes the filesystem.
  community.general.lvol:
    vg: ubuntu-vg
    lv: ubuntu-lv # Assumes a logical volume named 'ubuntu-lv' exists.
    size: +100%FREE
    resizefs: true
  become: true
  when: new_partition.changed
```

